1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,957
A household in Baku earns fifteen hundred manat this month. How

2
00:00:04,057 --> 00:00:07,498
much do we expect it to spend? The answer should depend

3
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:11,169
on the income: a richer household spends more. So what we

4
00:00:11,269 --> 00:00:14,711
want is not one number, but a whole function of income.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,466
That function is the conditional expectation, and it's the

6
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:23,517
idea behind every regression you will ever run.

7
00:00:24,616 --> 00:00:29,188
Here is a simple model. Income and spending, in thousands of manat,

8
00:00:29,288 --> 00:00:33,790
are spread evenly over a triangle: spending is never above income.

9
00:00:33,883 --> 00:00:38,105
Fix the income at fifteen hundred and look only at that thin

10
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:43,148
slice. Inside it, spending is uniform between zero and one point five.

11
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:47,471
So its average is the middle: seven hundred and fifty manat.

12
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:51,200
Do this for every income and the slice averages trace a

13
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:55,341
line: spending is half of income. Now notice: since income is

14
00:00:55,441 --> 00:00:59,822
random, this line evaluated at income is itself a random variable.

15
00:00:59,916 --> 00:01:04,013
And its average is the average spending. Average the slice averages,

16
00:01:04,113 --> 00:01:07,592
weighting each slice by how likely that income is, and you

17
00:01:07,692 --> 00:01:11,912
get about six hundred and sixty-seven manat. That's Theorem five point

18
00:01:12,012 --> 00:01:15,307
fourteen: the mean of the conditional mean is the mean.

19
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,080
For the bivariate normal, the slice averages always lie on

20
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:24,056
a straight line. Two stock indices with correlation zero: the

21
00:01:24,156 --> 00:01:27,055
cloud is round and the line is flat. Raise the

22
00:01:27,155 --> 00:01:30,118
correlation, and the cloud tilts onto the line.

23
00:01:30,716 --> 00:01:33,819
The slope of the line is rho times sigma one over

24
00:01:33,919 --> 00:01:38,068
sigma two. And the spread around it, the conditional variance, is

25
00:01:38,168 --> 00:01:42,382
sigma one squared times one minus rho squared. The more correlated

26
00:01:42,482 --> 00:01:45,192
the two, the better one predicts the other.

27
00:01:46,283 --> 00:01:51,150
Conditioning also splits risk. A pension fund's monthly return depends

28
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:54,982
on the regime. Eighty percent of months are calm: mean

29
00:01:55,082 --> 00:01:59,736
one point two percent, standard deviation three. Twenty percent are

30
00:01:59,836 --> 00:02:04,704
oil-price stress: mean minus two point five, standard deviation seven.

31
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,619
Theorem five point fifteen splits the total variance in two. The

32
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:12,784
within part is the average spread inside each regime: seventeen. The

33
00:02:12,884 --> 00:02:16,765
between part is how much the regime means themselves jump around:

34
00:02:16,865 --> 00:02:20,746
about two point two. Together, a standard deviation of four point

35
00:02:20,846 --> 00:02:24,666
four percent, well above the calm three percent. Not knowing the

36
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:26,381
regime is a risk of its own.

37
00:02:27,466 --> 00:02:32,147
So: the conditional expectation is the average inside each slice, and

38
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:37,066
it traces a regression line through the cloud. Averaging those averages

39
00:02:37,166 --> 00:02:41,362
gives back the ordinary mean. And total variance is the spread

40
00:02:41,462 --> 00:02:45,103
within the slices plus the spread between their means.

